trace.branch_2:
We:
\begin{align*}
(\sqrt{7} + \sqrt{5})^6 &= (\sqrt{7} + \sqrt{5})^2 \cdot (\sqrt{7} + \sqrt{5})^3 \\
&= (\sqrt{7} + \sqrt{5})^2 \cdot \left{ (\sqrt{7} + \sqrt{5})^2 \right) \\
&= 4{,}
\end{align*}
so the greatest integer less than 4 is $\boxed{3}$.Answer: 3

trace.branch_4:
The sum of the first $N$ positive odd numbers is $N^2$. Since $N^2 = 121$, we have that \begin{align*}
N^2 &= 121 \\
N &= \sqrt{121} \\
N &= 11.
\end{align*}
Answer: 11
